+# Privacy implementation and third-party audit
+
+## Scope and data inventory
+
+This repository contains a static home page and privacy notice, not an account,
+rewards, contact, or ordering backend. Existing service links are placeholders.
+No client-side script is necessary, so CSP blocks all scripts instead of adding
+JavaScript for consent, storage management, or tracking. No forms, browser
+permissions, cookies, local/session storage, service workers, analytics, or
+fingerprinting are used. No new personal data collection was introduced.
+
+| Component | Before | Current behavior / data exposure |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Hero photograph | Browser requested images.unsplash.com on each uncached visit | Reviewed JPEG copied to assets/coffee-hero.jpg; served by the site's own origin |
+| Styles and fonts | Inline CSS and system fonts | Local stylesheet and system fonts; no font CDN |
+| JavaScript, analytics, embeds | None | None; script-src, connect-src, and frame-src deny all |
+| Links | Browser's default referrer behavior | no-referrer in HTML and response headers; no tracking parameters or ping attributes |
+| Hosting | Deployment provider unspecified | Receives IP address, URL, and ordinary request headers to deliver pages; provider logging/retention must be configured by the operator |
+| Development server | pnpm dlx statikk | Existing developer-only tooling; not shipped in dist or required by the build; not a production security boundary |
+
+Photo source (downloaded October 28, 2026):
+https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1350&q=80
+The existing stock photograph is preserved; visitors no longer contact Unsplash.
+No third-party executable assets need SRI because none are loaded.
+
+## Deployment and security headers
+
+Run `npm run build` with Node.js 18 or newer. Deploy **dist/** only. The build
+copies an explicit allowlist, audits source and output, and includes `_headers`.
+`netlify.toml` configures this build and publish directory for Netlify. Hosts
+supporting Netlify-style `_headers` can consume the same file; on other hosts,
+translate its rules into actual response headers before deployment. Static
+servers such as the existing development command may ignore `_headers`.
+
+- CSP denies everything by default, permitting only same-origin images and
+  styles. Scripts, connections, forms, frames, plugins, and base URL changes are
+  blocked. Trusted Types is an additional defense in supporting browsers.
+- HTML includes an early CSP fallback and no-referrer before loading resources.
+  `frame-ancestors`, Permissions-Policy, HSTS, and other HTTP-only protections
+  require the host's response headers; a meta tag cannot replace them.
+- `frame-ancestors 'none'` and `X-Frame-Options: DENY` prevent framing.
+- Permissions-Policy disables unused device capabilities, payments, topics, and
+  attribution. Unknown directives are ignored by browsers; the site does not
+  rely on those APIs for privacy.
+- COOP uses same-origin-allow-popups; CORP uses same-origin. COEP is unnecessary
+  for this site and is intentionally omitted.
+- `nosniff` requires the host to serve HTML, CSS, and JPEG with correct MIME types.
+- HTTPS is required in production. HSTS starts at 300 seconds with no subdomain
+  or preload commitment. Increase its lifetime after confirming production HTTPS.
+- `Cache-Control: no-store` avoids persistent HTTP caching. It does not erase
+  existing cookies or storage and is not a logout mechanism.
+- No CSP reports are sent: there is no reviewed report receiver or retention
+  policy, and reports can themselves expose URLs and personal data.
+
+Reference: [Netlify static response headers](https://docs.netlify.com/manage/routing/headers/)
+apply to static assets, not proxied or function responses. Future API handlers
+must set their own headers. [MDN frame-ancestors documentation](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/frame-ancestors)
+explains why that directive is configured in HTTP headers rather than the meta fallback.
+
+## Repeatable audits
+
+Run `npm run audit:privacy`, `npm test`, and `npm run build`. These are offline
+source/build checks; they do not start a server or browser. The audit enforces the
+reviewed asset inventory, disallows scripts/forms/embeds/inline handlers and
+external references, checks referenced asset files, and checks privacy headers
+against the HTML fallback. Its conservative source checks are regression
+guardrails, not a general-purpose HTML parser or proof of deployed behavior.
+
+Repeat this review on every dependency, resource, or data-flow change and at
+least quarterly. Review every added vendor's purpose, request fields, cookies,
+subprocessors, retention/deletion terms, permissions, and supply-chain update
+policy before integration. Prefer self-hosted assets and ordinary links. If an
+embed is necessary, load it only after a clear user action and explanation;
+review even its placeholder image for third-party requests. Immutable external
+scripts/styles need pinned versions, SRI, anonymous CORS, and no-referrer.
+
+No browser or deployed-network verification was performed for this change.
+Source audits cannot detect host-injected analytics, CDN transformations, or
+hosting logs. The deployment operator must confirm the response headers are
+applied to pages, assets, redirects, and errors, disable provider-injected
+analytics, and review hosting logs before claiming end-to-end privacy. Keep
+raw IP addresses and request headers out of application logs where possible;
+redact query strings and identifiers, restrict log access, and set the shortest
+documented retention needed for security. The actual hosting provider and its
+retention configuration remain deployment decisions, not guarantees in this code.
+
+## Requirements before adding accounts or checkout
+
+These controls require a backend and are not implemented by static markup:
+
+- Offer guest checkout; request only fields needed for fulfillment and explain
+  each purpose beside the field. Separate optional marketing consent, default it
+  off, and never reuse order/security data for marketing without consent.
+- Validate and authorize all requests server-side; bound inputs and rate-limit
+  sensitive operations. Keep PII, payment details, credentials, session IDs, and
+  tokens out of URLs, client storage, diagnostics, and logs. Use provider-hosted
+  payment collection rather than retaining card details.
+- Use HTTPS and server-issued sessions such as
+  `__Host-session=…; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/`, with no Domain.
+  Rotate session IDs and expire sessions server-side. Do not emulate HttpOnly
+  cookies or token security with client-side JavaScript.
+- Replace the placeholder logout link with a CSRF-protected POST. Invalidate
+  the server session, expire its cookie, then return
+  `Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache"` on the successful logout
+  response only. Clear-Site-Data needs HTTPS and cannot replace server revocation.
+  Do not place that header on all pages or a static fake logout endpoint.
+- Define retention and automated deletion for each stored field and backups;
+  support authenticated access, correction, export, and deletion with simple
+  flows. Apply least privilege and encryption at rest to server records.
+- Update the public privacy notice with the actual processor, purposes,
+  retention, and rights channel before collection starts. Re-audit CSP and
+  permissions narrowly for any new capability rather than adding wildcards or
+  unsafe-inline. Any telemetry receiver needs PII scrubbing and retention limits.
